  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "L1 gold-context records report measured human absolute oral bioavailability values predominantly in the 50-100% range, all above the F>=20% threshold: doxazosin 62-75%, ACT-1004-1239 53%, suvorexant 82%, ofloxacin 80-95%, tegafur ~100%, nebivolol 96% in extensive metabolizers, balcinrenone 52%, dovitinib ~75%, mirtazapine ~50%, and baloxavir marboxil high.",
      "card_ids": ["C01", "C04", "C05", "C06", "C08", "C09", "C10", "C11", "C12", "C15", "C17", "C19", "C21", "C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 provides a direct oral-versus-IV comparison for tedizolid (oral AUC 26.1 vs IV 30.5 µg·h/mL; oral Cmax 2.25 vs IV 3.02 µg/mL), indicating high oral bioavailability for that reference, and other L3 records show measurable oral Cmax/AUC for related polar drugs.",
      "card_ids": ["C89", "C107", "C70", "C80"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 adds favorable solubility/dissolution evidence for polar analogs, including fexuprazan (a sulfonyl-pyrazole/difluorophenyl compound) freely soluble at pH 4.0, PNU96391 with >90% GI absorption in rats, and verubecestat as BCS class 1 with very rapid dissolution.",
      "card_ids": ["C116", "C147", "C146", "C159"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 shows that several structurally related difluorophenyl/pyrazole/basic-amine molecules are P-gp/BCRP substrates and that efflux inhibition increases exposure (repotrectinib, larotrectinib, crizotinib, JNJ-7706621, EAI045, ofloxacin), but none provides a direct F<20% outcome for the query; ofloxacin has measured F 80-95% despite P-gp-sensitive intestinal secretion, and crizotinib's effect is dose-dependent without a sub-20% F value.",
      "card_ids": ["C196", "C202", "C188", "C213", "C179", "C215", "C182", "C212", "C166", "C195", "C19"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F<20% for the query; the physicochemical permeability liability is generic and L5 efflux mechanism alone is insufficient to establish a sub-20% outcome.",
      "card_ids": []
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "gut_wall_efflux_intestinal_metabolism",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C196", "C202", "C188", "C213", "C179", "C215", "C182", "C212", "C166", "C203", "C192", "C183", "C177", "C186", "C195", "C201", "C211", "C189"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct human or preclinical PK/bioavailability measurement for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific oral AUC, Cmax, or IV-comparator data.",
    "No query-specific permeability, efflux, transporter, or intestinal-metabolism assay; L5 records are for related but not identical molecules.",
    "No dose, formulation, or species context for the query.",
    "Unknown metabolic stability and first-pass extraction for the query.",
    "L5 record transfer likelihoods are moderate-low (~0.49-0.58), limiting direct extrapolation."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L1/L2 gold-context records predominantly support F>=20%, with most measured human values in the 50-100% range. L3 adds a direct oral-versus-IV comparison for tedizolid and measurable oral exposure for related drugs. L4 adds favorable solubility/dissolution evidence, including a sulfonyl-pyrazole/difluorophenyl analog (fexuprazan) reported freely soluble. L5 introduces a mechanistic gut-wall efflux concern for structurally related molecules, but no direct sub-20% F outcome for the query, and examples such as ofloxacin show efflux can coexist with F>=20%. The high prediction is retained with moderate confidence; the L5 efflux evidence modestly weakens but does not overturn the prior decision."
}

